# shell: desktop
# AW-030: a double click on a file that can be EXECUTED starts it.
#
# The registry answers which application opens a document.  Asked about a
# program, it has nothing to say -- nothing "opens" an executable -- and
# the shell used to stop there and report that no application was found,
# with a runnable file sitting under the pointer.  A program needs no
# opener: it is one.
#
# Proved by effect rather than by appearance: the script fixture plants a
# runnable that leaves a file behind, and the check is that the file is
# there.  A launch that only looked like it happened would pass a check
# on the window and fail this one.
wait 14
note the runnable sits after the folder and the three text files
click-row 4
wait 3
expect-selected-name runme
note a double click starts it, and it leaves its mark
click-row 4
click-row 4
wait 40
expect-file /tmp/trk_ran
quit
